@startuml
title UML sequence - exact trade approval, reservation and native financial receipt
actor Customer
participant "Official portal" as UI
participant "Customer BFF" as API
participant "OIDC issuer" as OIDC
participant "Core capability" as Core
database "Native financial schemas" as DB
participant "Recovery worker" as Worker
Customer -> UI : Choose BUY/SELL, amount/shares, market/limit, TIF
UI -> API : POST investments/quotes (session + CSRF)
API -> Core : Current canonical actor and exact terms
Core -> DB : Lock current person; validate cash / shares; retain full quote + digest
DB --> UI : Immutable authorization, expiry and issuer approval URL
Customer -> UI : Confirm identity for these exact terms
UI -> API : Open challenge URL
API -> OIDC : Authorization code + PKCE, fresh login, nonce and exact subject
OIDC --> API : Subject-bound fresh authentication
API -> DB : Retain matching expiring action-bound proof
API --> UI : Return to saved review route
Customer -> UI : Submit approved instruction
UI -> API : Original UUID key + authorizationId only
API -> Core : Execute exact approved instruction
Core -> DB : Person lock; replay original receipt or reject key/input mismatch
Core -> DB : Validate fresh matching proof and one authorization / command
Core -> DB : Reserve native cash or unreserved microshares
alt Price satisfies approved bound
 Core -> DB : Balanced native journal + position event + fill + outbox + original command receipt (atomic)
 DB --> UI : FILLED + actual batch, units, price and cash
else Limit remains open
 Core -> DB : Retain OPEN order and original reservation
 DB --> UI : OPEN; no fill receipt
 Worker -> Core : Recover owned open order or expire
 Core -> DB : Revalidate native authority, current price and exact original reservation
end
note over Core,DB
Cancellation releases only an OPEN order's original reservation.
A request-key replay returns its original financial effect.
Reusing the authorization under a new key is refused.
TST assets and prices are explicitly synthetic.
end note
@enduml
